---
/**
 * What is actually deployed, drawn from the two wrangler.jsonc files and the
 * two Worker entries rather than from a sketch.
 *
 * IT DISAGREES WITH 01 §2's ASCII DRAWING, DELIBERATELY. That diagram puts the
 * chat and analysis endpoints on the site Worker; they are on the MCP Worker,
 * because both need the `ai` binding and an `ai` binding cannot exist in the
 * config `astro build` instantiates (the CI failure is written up in both
 * wrangler.jsonc files). Publishing the spec's version on the page whose whole
 * premise is that its figures are checkable would be self-refuting -- the first
 * reader to `curl` the two endpoints would find the picture wrong.
 *
 * TWO CLAIMS IN THE FIRST DRAFT OF THIS FILE CAME FROM THAT SAME STALE DRAWING
 * AND WERE FALSE. Recorded rather than quietly replaced, because both are the
 * kind of detail a reader would actually reason from:
 *
 *   - "`/mcp` is a Durable Object" -- IT IS NOT. The endpoint is served by
 *     `createMcpHandler` (agents/mcp/server), which this repo's own comments
 *     twice describe as stateless: workers/mcp/src/index.ts builds "one
 *     instance per HTTP request", and workers/mcp/src/define.ts's whole
 *     `clientInfo` argument exists BECAUSE the server is rebuilt per request.
 *     That Worker declares exactly one Durable Object class and it is the rate
 *     limiter (workers/mcp/wrangler.jsonc's `RATE_LIMITER`/`RateLimiter`,
 *     sqlite migration v1, re-exported from index.ts). The rate limiter now has
 *     its own line, which is both true and the more interesting fact.
 *   - "Analytics Engine feeds the events queue" -- IT DOES NOT. They are two
 *     independent sinks written from the same handlers: AE through
 *     `env.AE.writeDataPoint` (src/lib/agent-intel/record.ts, fire and forget),
 *     the queue through `env.EVENTS.send` on the high-intent path
 *     (src/worker.ts, via `highIntentFor`). The consumer
 *     (src/lib/agent-intel/consume.ts) reads queue message bodies and never
 *     touches AE, and nothing in either Worker reads AE at all --
 *     src/lib/ops/analytics.ts's header says that binding "writes and cannot
 *     query", which is why /ops reads it over HTTPS instead.
 *
 * Every other claim re-verified 2026-09-11 against ./wrangler.jsonc,
 * workers/mcp/wrangler.jsonc, src/worker.ts, workers/mcp/src/index.ts,
 * src/pages/chat/send.ts, migrations/0001-0004 and the five model call sites
 * (src/lib/{chat,fit,judge}/engine.ts, src/lib/corpus.ts, src/lib/mcp/search.ts).
 *
 * THE TWO INFERENCE LINES NAME PATHS, NOT EXCLUSIONS. Saying so because the
 * first draft of this comment offered a reason for them and the reason was
 * wrong twice over -- it claimed "two" calls pass a `gateway:` option (there
 * are THREE: chat, fit, and the judge in src/lib/judge/engine.ts, which is
 * deployed and scope-gated), and it claimed the embedding path "carries no
 * `gateway:` option, which is why only the Anthropic line names one" -- which
 * implies embeddings bypass the gateway.
 *
 * THEY DO NOT, and this repo had already measured it. `env.AI.run()` bills
 * through AI Gateway's UNIFIED BILLING whether or not a call names the gateway
 * -- day 1's measurement, `gatewayMetadata.keySource: "Unified"`, recorded in
 * src/lib/judge/engine.ts and evals/README.md (10 §5). A `gateway:` option buys
 * attribution in the gateway's own logs (each call tags a `surface`), not a
 * different route. Writing a causal story from the option's presence was
 * exactly the failure this diagram's own corrections are about.
 *
 * What the two lines distinguish is the MODEL, and neither is a statement about
 * the other: `AI Gateway -> Anthropic` is the third-party frontier route,
 * `Workers AI -> Vectorize` the first-party embedding model feeding the vector
 * index.
 *
 * NO UNLISTED ROUTE IS NAMED HERE, and that is the one thing this diagram
 * deliberately leaves out. src/worker.ts flattens every refusal on the
 * token-scoped prefix into the site's own 404 precisely so that a stranger
 * cannot tell a real route from a dead one, src/lib/unindexed-routes.mjs keeps
 * it out of the sitemap and src/pages/llms.txt.ts out of the index -- and /ops
 * IS in the sitemap. Printing that path here would spend a documented
 * requirement and three mechanisms for one word on a drawing. "the form
 * surfaces" is the honest aggregate.
 *
 * IT WAS AN INLINE SVG UNTIL THE 2026-09 REDESIGN AND IS NOW DOM, and the
 * handoff's reason is one this file had already half-argued for itself. The
 * SVG's own header said "INLINE SVG, NEVER AN `<img>`: the diagram has to
 * follow the visitor's theme, and an image cannot." The redesign keeps that
 * requirement and adds one an SVG could not meet: the diagram has to stay
 * SELECTABLE TEXT, so that a reader can drag across `mcp.ryanlindsey.me` and
 * paste it. SVG `<text>` selects inconsistently across browsers and never
 * reflows. Boxes and rules in DOM do both, at every width.
 *
 * TWO CONSTRAINTS DIED WITH THE SVG, RECORDED SO NOBODY REDERIVES THEM. The
 * drawing scaled rather than scrolled (`w-full` over a `viewBox`), so every
 * label shrank with it, and the old header carried the arithmetic that kept
 * labels above `--text-micro`: a 400px viewport gave the svg 352px, so a user
 * unit rendered at 0.88px and nothing could be set below 12/0.88 = 13.6, hence
 * 14 for every label and 17 for the box titles. It also bounded every line to
 * 40 characters, because IBM Plex Mono advances 0.6em and a 14-unit line inside
 * a 360-wide box fits no more. NEITHER SURVIVES THE PORT: type comes from the
 * named scale and text wraps, so the lines below are as long as reads well
 * rather than as long as fits.
 *
 * IT IS A CONTAINER QUERY AND NOT A MEDIA QUERY, which is not a style
 * preference. This component has two call sites whose columns differ by half
 * at the SAME viewport: /ops gives it the full content width, and
 * src/content/posts/agent-native-site.mdx renders it inside `prose-rl`, capped
 * at 72ch. A `lg:` variant would put the two Workers side by side in that prose
 * column, about 220px each, and every route line would wrap to three. The
 * container is the only thing that can tell the two call sites apart.
 *
 * `not-prose` IS FOR THE SAME TWO CALL SITES. Inside agent-native-site.mdx this
 * figure lands in a `.prose-rl` column, and Tailwind Typography then styles the
 * markup it finds: MEASURED before the class went on, the route rows rendered
 * with accent bullets and a hanging indent inside the Worker boxes, which /ops
 * never showed because nothing there is prose. Every element below is styled by
 * utility and token already, so opting the subtree out is the whole fix.
 *
 * THE ACCESSIBLE NAME AND DESCRIPTION ARE ELEMENTS NOW, not `<title>`/`<desc>`.
 * The SVG carried `role="img"` so a screen reader read those two strings and
 * skipped the 40-odd shapes underneath. There are no shapes to skip any more,
 * and the box text is real text worth reading, so the figure keeps the same two
 * strings as visually-hidden elements: `aria-labelledby` for the title, so the
 * figure announces as "How this site is deployed", and `aria-describedby` for
 * the prose. The connectors
 * and the arrow glyphs are `aria-hidden`: they are how the layout says "calls",
 * and the description already says it in words.
 *
 * THE PROSE MOVED OUT, and the two audiences for it are the reason. An SVG was
 * nothing at all in the markdown src/lib/markdown-export.ts produces, so when
 * agent-native-site.mdx started using this component, every consumer of the
 * portable document got a hole where the architecture was. The `<desc>` string
 * was already the best prose statement of this architecture in the repo and was
 * reaching screen readers only, so it became src/lib/architecture.ts and the
 * exporter substitutes it for the tag. That substitution is untouched by this
 * rewrite: the exporter strips the component tag, so what the tag rendered into
 * never mattered to it. Do NOT re-inline either string here: a copy is how the
 * drawing and the markdown start disagreeing, which is the failure the two
 * corrections above are already about. tests/markdown-export.test.ts asserts
 * this file imports rather than inlines.
 */
import { ARCHITECTURE_DESCRIPTION, ARCHITECTURE_TITLE } from '../lib/architecture';

/**
 * One Worker box. `routes` is what the box lists under its header, in the order
 * a request meets them rather than the order the config declares them.
 *
 * THE MCP BOX KEEPS ITS DISCOVERY ROUTES, which the handoff's screenshot does
 * not show. Same argument the /ops issue makes about that page's six sections:
 * the design shows what fitted in a screenshot. `/robots.txt` and
 * `/.well-known/mcp.json` are served by that Worker today, are named in
 * ARCHITECTURE_DESCRIPTION, and were on the drawing this replaces, so dropping
 * the line to match a picture would be a content edit wearing a restyle.
 */
const WORKERS: { number: string; role: string; host: string; what: string; routes: string[] }[] = [
  {
    number: '02',
    role: 'Site Worker',
    host: 'ryanlindsey.me',
    what: 'Astro, prerendered with some routes rendered on demand',
    routes: [
      'pages, posts, case studies, the résumé',
      'llms.txt, .md variants, RSS and JSON',
      'chat page and the form surfaces',
      'queue consumer and the email send',
    ],
  },
  {
    number: '03',
    role: 'MCP Worker',
    host: 'mcp.ryanlindsey.me',
    what: 'Everything that spends inference, plus the one Durable Object',
    routes: [
      '/mcp endpoint, stateless per request',
      'POST /chat, the streamed inference',
      '/robots.txt, /.well-known/mcp.json',
      'rate limits in a Durable Object',
      'AI Gateway → Anthropic',
      'Workers AI → Vectorize, for retrieval',
    ],
  },
];

/** The five bindings both Workers hold, and what each one is for. */
const BINDINGS: { name: string; what: string }[] = [
  { name: 'D1', what: 'audit · tokens · transcripts · evals' },
  { name: 'KV', what: 'caches and flags' },
  { name: 'R2', what: 'generated PDFs and documents' },
  { name: 'Analytics Engine', what: 'request telemetry' },
  { name: 'Queue', what: 'high-intent events → email' },
];

/**
 * The three-column track the whole flow aligns to: box, gutter, box.
 *
 * One constant rather than four copies, because the connectors only read as
 * connectors while they land on the centre of the box beneath them, and that
 * only holds while every row declares the same tracks.
 */
const FLOW = '@3xl:grid-cols-[minmax(0,1fr)_168px_minmax(0,1fr)]';
---

<figure
  data-architecture-diagram
  aria-labelledby="architecture-title"
  aria-describedby="architecture-desc"
  class="not-prose @container mt-10"
>
  <p id="architecture-title" class="sr-only">
    {ARCHITECTURE_TITLE}
  </p>
  <p id="architecture-desc" class="sr-only">
    {ARCHITECTURE_DESCRIPTION}
  </p>

  {/*
      01, the ingress. The one row spanning the whole width, because everything
      below it is downstream of the same door.
    */}
  <div class="flex items-baseline justify-between gap-4 border border-rule bg-surface px-[18px] py-3.5">
    <p class="flex gap-3 font-mono text-micro uppercase text-ink">
      <span class="text-ink-muted">01</span>
      <span>Visitors and agents</span>
    </p>
    <p class="font-mono text-micro uppercase text-accent">HTTPS</p>
  </div>

  {/*
      The accent connectors, centred on the Worker box each one lands on. Pure
      decoration: the description says "reach the site Worker" in words, and a
      screen reader gets nothing from a 1px rule.
    */}
  <div class:list={['grid grid-cols-1', FLOW]} aria-hidden="true">
    <span class="mx-auto block h-8 w-px bg-accent"></span>
    <span class="hidden @3xl:block"></span>
    <span class="mx-auto hidden h-8 w-px bg-accent @3xl:block"></span>
  </div>

  <div class:list={['grid grid-cols-1', FLOW]}>
    <div class="border border-ink bg-surface">
      <div class="px-[18px] pb-4 pt-[15px]">
        <p class="font-mono text-micro uppercase text-accent">
          {WORKERS[0].number} · {WORKERS[0].role}
        </p>
        <p class="mt-2.5 font-display text-subheading text-ink">{WORKERS[0].host}</p>
        <p class="mt-1.5 text-small text-ink-muted">{WORKERS[0].what}</p>
      </div>
      <ul class="border-t border-rule">
        {WORKERS[0].routes.map((route) => (
          <li class="border-b border-rule px-[18px] py-[11px] font-mono text-small text-ink last:border-b-0">
            {route}
          </li>
        ))}
      </ul>
    </div>

    {/*
        The gutter, and it is the middle grid item in both layouts rather than
        two copies behind a breakpoint. Stacked it falls between the two boxes
        on its own, which is where it belongs and what it says; side by side it
        is the 168px track. The two labels are content -- they name what crosses
        the binding -- while the rules and the glyphs are not.

        THE ARROWS ARE THE EXCEPTION, and issue #113 calls it correctly: this is
        a content problem wearing a layout problem's clothes. The labels survive
        the stack unchanged because "/mcp · chat · forms" names the traffic
        whichever way the boxes sit. `▶` does not. Stacked, the site Worker is
        ABOVE and the MCP Worker BELOW, so that traffic runs down and the
        document reads run back up, and an arrow pointing right says neither.

        So the connectors turn with the layout: a vertical rule into a `▼` and a
        `▲` into a vertical rule while stacked, the original horizontal pair at
        `@3xl`. Both pairs ship and the container query picks one, which is the
        same mechanism the rest of this component already uses -- and a
        container query rather than a media query for the reason the file header
        gives, that /ops and agent-native-site.mdx render this at widths that
        differ by half at the same viewport.
      */}
    <div data-binding-gutter class="flex flex-col justify-center gap-1.5 py-6 @3xl:px-5 @3xl:py-0">
      <p class="text-center font-mono text-micro text-ink-muted">/mcp · chat · forms</p>
      <p class="flex flex-col items-center @3xl:hidden">
        <span aria-hidden="true" class="h-6 w-px bg-accent"></span>
        <span aria-hidden="true" class="-mt-px leading-none text-accent">
          ▼
        </span>
      </p>
      <p class="hidden items-center @3xl:flex">
        <span aria-hidden="true" class="h-px flex-1 bg-accent"></span>
        <span aria-hidden="true" class="-ml-px leading-none text-accent">
          ▶
        </span>
      </p>
      <p class="flex flex-col items-center @3xl:hidden">
        <span aria-hidden="true" class="-mb-px leading-none text-accent">
          ▲
        </span>
        <span aria-hidden="true" class="h-6 w-px bg-accent"></span>
      </p>
      <p class="hidden items-center @3xl:flex">
        <span aria-hidden="true" class="-mr-px leading-none text-accent">
          ◀
        </span>
        <span aria-hidden="true" class="h-px flex-1 bg-accent"></span>
      </p>
      <p class="text-center font-mono text-micro text-ink-muted">document reads</p>
    </div>

    <div class="border border-ink bg-surface">
      <div class="px-[18px] pb-4 pt-[15px]">
        <p class="font-mono text-micro uppercase text-accent">
          {WORKERS[1].number} · {WORKERS[1].role}
        </p>
        <p class="mt-2.5 font-display text-subheading text-ink">{WORKERS[1].host}</p>
        <p class="mt-1.5 text-small text-ink-muted">{WORKERS[1].what}</p>
      </div>
      <ul class="border-t border-rule">
        {WORKERS[1].routes.map((route) => (
          <li class="border-b border-rule px-[18px] py-[11px] font-mono text-small text-ink last:border-b-0">
            {route}
          </li>
        ))}
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>

  {/*
      The muted connectors down into the bindings, quieter than the accent pair
      above because a binding is not a request.
    */}
  <div class:list={['grid grid-cols-1', FLOW]} aria-hidden="true">
    <span class="mx-auto block h-8 w-px bg-rule"></span>
    <span class="hidden @3xl:block"></span>
    <span class="mx-auto hidden h-8 w-px bg-rule @3xl:block"></span>
  </div>

  <div class="border border-rule bg-surface px-[18px] py-3.5">
    <p class="font-mono text-micro uppercase text-ink-muted">
      04 · Shared by both Workers · bindings
    </p>
  </div>
  {/*
      hairline-grid owns the ground, the gap and the border; the cell picks its
      own background, and --rl-surface is the one that matches the boxes above.

      FIVE COLUMNS OR ONE, NEVER A COUNT IN BETWEEN. Five is prime, so any other
      track count leaves the last row short, and a short row in a hairline grid
      is not empty space: the container's own --rl-rule ground paints straight
      through it as a block of grey. The same arithmetic governs the metrics
      band on /ops, which is why that one is ten cells over five columns.

      `-mt-px` laps this grid's top border over the bar's bottom one, so the two
      meet as the single hairline the idiom is named for rather than as 2px.
    */}
  <div class="hairline-grid -mt-px grid-cols-1 @3xl:grid-cols-5">
    {BINDINGS.map((binding) => (
      <div class="bg-surface px-[18px] py-3.5">
        <p class="font-mono text-small text-ink">{binding.name}</p>
        <p class="mt-1.5 font-mono text-micro uppercase text-ink-muted">{binding.what}</p>
      </div>
    ))}
  </div>
</figure>
